---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About - HackFinder" description="The story behind HackFinder — why it was built, the tech stack, and the developer behind it.">

  <!-- Hero / Intro -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-br from-indigo-950/50 via-gray-950 to-purple-950/30" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 sm:py-28 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
        About <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-indigo-400 to-purple-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">HackFinder</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-300">
        A hackathon discovery platform born out of frustration, built with modern tools, and deployed at the edge.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: Why I Built This -->
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
    <h2 id="why-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-3xl">
      Why I Built HackFinder
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        As someone who regularly participates in hackathons, I found the experience of discovering events frustrating. Hackathons are scattered across Devpost, MLH, HackerEarth, and other platforms — each with their own interface, their own search quirks, and no way to see everything in one view.
      </p>
      <p>
        I kept missing deadlines or discovering great events too late because there was no single source of truth. HackFinder solves that by aggregating hackathon data from multiple platforms into one searchable, filterable interface.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond solving a real problem, this project also serves as a portfolio piece demonstrating full-stack development, edge computing with Cloudflare, and modern web architecture — all built from scratch using a spec-driven workflow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: How It Was Made -->
  <section class="border-y border-gray-800 bg-gray-900/30" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 id="how-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-3xl">
        How It Was Made
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg font-medium text-indigo-400">Built with Kiro — an AI-powered IDE</p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        This project was built using Kiro's spec-driven workflow, which guided the entire process from requirements gathering to deployment. Here's how it came together:
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6" role="list">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-600/20 ring-1 ring-indigo-500/30 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-400">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Requirements</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">Defined 8 requirements covering data aggregation, search, filtering, responsive UI, SEO, and deployment.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-600/20 ring-1 ring-indigo-500/30 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-400">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Design</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">Architected the system with Astro + Cloudflare Pages/Workers/D1, an adapter pattern for data sources, and FTS5 for search.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-600/20 ring-1 ring-indigo-500/30 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-400">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Task Breakdown</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">83 tasks organized into waves, each building on the previous — from schema design through UI polish.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-600/20 ring-1 ring-indigo-500/30 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-400">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Implementation</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">Code generated and verified task-by-task with 230 unit tests ensuring correctness at every step.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-600/20 ring-1 ring-indigo-500/30 text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-400">5</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-white">Deployment</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-400">Deployed to Cloudflare's edge network for global performance — entirely on the free tier.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <!-- Kiro callout -->
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-indigo-500/30 bg-indigo-950/30 p-6 sm:p-8">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-indigo-300">
          Want to build something like this?
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-gray-300">
          Check out <a href="https://kiro.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="font-semibold text-indigo-400 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-indigo-300 transition-colors">Kiro</a> — the AI-powered development environment that guided this entire build from requirements to deployment.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Naming decision -->
      <div class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-gray-800 bg-gray-900/50 p-6">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-200">A note on naming</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          The product is called <strong class="text-gray-200">HackFinder</strong> in the UI — short, punchy, and clean in a nav bar. The repo and subdomain use <strong class="text-gray-200">hackathon-finder</strong> — the full word helps with SEO (people search "hackathon finder", not "hackfinder") and makes the URL instantly clear to anyone who sees it. This is the same pattern real products use: a branded name for the interface, the descriptive name for discovery.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

